rable midterm environment in an even-lean open seat with Evers retiring", "Tiffany's roughly ten-to-one fundraising and cash-on-hand advantage", "Prediction markets price Crowley near 80 percent while raters split between Tossup and Lean D"], "rationale": "Polling across the spectrum, including Republican-sponsored surveys, consistently places Crowley up three to four points, and markets price his win odds near 80 percent. Tiffany's large financial edge and Wisconsin's even partisan balance keep this well short of Likely D.", "watch": "A late advertising surge funded by Tiffany's cash advantage, or a late national break toward Republicans, could erase the roughly three-point gap.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Open seat: Tony Evers (D) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Wisconsin's governor race is an open contest between Democrat David Crowley and Republican Tom Tiffany following Tony Evers's retirement. Polling since midsummer has steadily shown Crowley ahead, with margins between one and five points in surveys from Marquette, YouGov, and others, including polls sponsored by Republican-leaning firms. Aggregators place the race around three to four points in Crowley's favor, and prediction markets price his chances near eighty percent.\n\nTiffany holds a large financial edge, having raised roughly ten times more than Crowley, and that advantage could weigh on the final stretch. Expert raters are split between tossup and lean-D ratings, reflecting the state's even partisan balance. The single debate in late October featured clashes on taxes, abortion, and vouchers but appears not to have reshaped the contest. The race remains competitive but tilts toward Crowley.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.7127, "margin": 2.83}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.7543, "margin": 3.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.7549, "margin": 3.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.7725, "margin": 3.8}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.7589, "margin": 4.0}, {"d